{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And YHWH spoke to Moses and to Aaron, saying to them.</b> The word 'to them' requires explanation, for what Rashi explained — that it refers back to Eleazar and Ithamar, that they too should be commissioned in this commandment because all of them were equated in silence — is not clear, since Eleazar and Ithamar were not mentioned before this. Moreover the verse says 'and Aaron was silent' but not Eleazar and Ithamar. And what seems right to me to say is that in the word 'to them' there is hinted a matter of great preciousness, and it comes to exclude all the nations. And it is in the manner that many of the commentators wrote concerning the matter of the dietary prohibitions, that some thought they are for the health of bodies and their healing, for those foods produce bad humors, and to this opinion the Ramban inclined. But this is not so, for behold all the nations eat the flesh of the abomination and the rabbit and all the unclean ones, and all of them are strong and healthy, they do not stumble in them, and those who eat them do not become ill nor feel anything." "Rather, it is certain that the intent in all of these is only for the healing of the soul. For they sicken the pure soul and drive away the spirit of purity and holiness from a person, and they produce dullness of the intellect and the disposition of cruelty — thus wrote the Akeidah and the Maharik. And the proof for this is that a cow born from a donkey is forbidden, for that which comes forth from the unclean is unclean — infer from this that the producer imparts its bad disposition to that which is born from it. And along these lines it is said 'Nevertheless these you shall not eat, of those that chew the cud and of those that divide the hoof,' and the mem of 'and of those that divide' is superfluous, for it should have said 'of those that chew the cud and those that divide the hoof'; rather, as the earlier commentators explained, 'these you shall not eat' even if it has all the signs of purity — that is, if it is born and comes from a cud-chewer alone, such as a cow from a camel, or from a hoof-divider alone, that is, a cow from a pig. And likewise they gave reasons for all the signs of impurity, that all of them indicate the coarseness of the flesh and the bad temperament in it, which produces bad dispositions in the body of the eater, and there is no need to elaborate on this. And concerning this it is said (Job 14:4): 'Who can bring a clean thing out of an unclean?' — and is it possible that from an unclean thing a clean one should emerge? And are not the producer and the produced one and the same, for the fetus is the thigh of its father." "And this is what is concluded in Vayikra Rabbah (13:2): A parable of a physician who entered to visit two sick persons, etc. — thus the nations, who are not for the life of the world to come, like greens of the field He permitted everything to them; but Israel, who are for the life of the world to come, He commanded them 'this is the living creature that you may eat.' And perhaps for this reason it says 'this is the living creature' even though it concludes with 'animal' — nevertheless it began with 'living creature' to say that all these restrictions are for the sake of the soul, which is called 'living creature.' Behold, this midrash agrees from every side with our words, for if not so, what did he see to give a reason for this commandment — why was it not given to the nations more than any other commandment in the Torah? Rather, it is certain that he wanted to resolve the superfluity of the expression 'to them,' which comes to exclude the nations, and to tell you that presumably no gentile wants to fulfill any commandment unless he knows that there is benefit in it also in this world, since in any case they have no share in the world to come, as it is written (Deuteronomy 7:10): 'and repays them that hate Him to their face, to destroy them.' And in order that the nations not err by thinking that the reason for the dietary prohibitions is for the health of bodies and they too might wish to conduct themselves according to the medical reasoning, therefore it says 'to them' — to tell you that specifically for Israel, who are for the life of the world to come, is there benefit in this commandment; but for the nations there is no benefit in keeping this commandment, for in any case they are not for the life of the world to come."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:35:52.666847050Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}